WEBVTT

00:00.980 --> 00:04.678
A factor model fits stock returns with three kinds of pieces:

00:04.718 --> 00:05.952
observed factors,

00:05.992 --> 00:06.940
latent factors,

00:06.980 --> 00:08.550
and an intercept.

00:08.590 --> 00:09.825
It fits their sum.

00:09.865 --> 00:12.360
The data do not say how to split it.

00:12.400 --> 00:13.973
Splitting it needs a ruler.

00:14.013 --> 00:16.310
Here are seven questions a committee would ask,

00:16.350 --> 00:18.155
and the paper's answers.

00:21.835 --> 00:22.695
Question one.

00:22.735 --> 00:24.280
What is the ruler?

00:24.320 --> 00:27.705
A rule for how large a combination of characteristics is,

00:27.745 --> 00:30.515
and how much two combinations overlap.

00:30.555 --> 00:33.253
The usual ruler compares their weights on paper.

00:33.293 --> 00:36.040
Book-to-market and earnings-to-price share no weights,

00:36.080 --> 00:38.850
so on paper their overlap is zero.

00:38.890 --> 00:41.313
The paper's ruler applies both to the stocks,

00:41.353 --> 00:42.950
multiplies stock by stock,

00:42.990 --> 00:44.213
and averages.

00:44.253 --> 00:45.675
In this made-up example,

00:45.715 --> 00:47.695
the two are 0.8 aligned.

00:48.375 --> 00:49.310
Question two.

00:49.350 --> 00:51.595
Isn't that just a correlation?

00:51.635 --> 00:52.032
Yes.

00:52.072 --> 00:54.407
And it is already inside the estimator.

00:54.447 --> 00:57.732
Each month, the latent factor returns come from a regression

00:57.772 --> 01:00.380
weighted by exactly this matrix.

01:00.420 --> 01:02.068
The ruler is familiar.

01:02.108 --> 01:03.593
What is new is what it shows

01:03.633 --> 01:05.675
when you use it to read the model.

01:06.355 --> 01:07.340
Question three.

01:07.380 --> 01:10.175
Why does a factor model need a ruler at all?

01:10.215 --> 01:13.712
Because the latent factors can absorb any part of the intercept,

01:13.752 --> 01:15.700
or of the observed-factor loadings,

01:15.740 --> 01:17.885
that lies in their span.

01:17.925 --> 01:19.297
Move that part across,

01:19.337 --> 01:21.835
adjust the latent factor returns to match,

01:21.875 --> 01:24.770
and every fitted return is unchanged.

01:24.810 --> 01:26.845
So the data pin down the latent span,

01:26.885 --> 01:29.457
and the other two blocks only up to that span.

01:29.497 --> 01:31.490
A ruler picks one split.

01:32.145 --> 01:33.005
Question four.

01:33.045 --> 01:35.130
IPCA has latent factors.

01:35.170 --> 01:37.490
Why study three blocks together?

01:37.530 --> 01:38.990
With latent factors alone,

01:39.030 --> 01:41.665
the only freedom is rotation and units.

01:41.705 --> 01:43.700
There is nothing to split.

01:43.740 --> 01:48.025
The split appears once observed factors or an intercept sit beside them.

01:48.065 --> 01:50.387
And that is exactly when researchers ask:

01:50.427 --> 01:51.625
is alpha zero?

01:51.665 --> 01:53.600
Do the latent factors add anything?

01:53.640 --> 01:55.195
Who gets the credit?

01:55.875 --> 01:56.860
Question five.

01:56.900 --> 01:57.647
Why this ruler,

01:57.687 --> 01:59.095
and not another?

01:59.135 --> 02:02.345
Any positive-definite matrix gives a valid split.

02:02.385 --> 02:05.980
The paper gives three reasons for the characteristic one.

02:06.020 --> 02:06.442
One.

02:06.482 --> 02:09.805
Orthogonal weights do not make orthogonal stock positions.

02:09.845 --> 02:11.155
Under the usual ruler,

02:11.195 --> 02:15.090
the intercept can still overlap with the loadings across stocks.

02:15.130 --> 02:15.540
Two.

02:15.580 --> 02:17.740
It is the estimator's own metric.

02:17.780 --> 02:22.400
On average, the latent factor returns stop picking up the other two blocks.

02:22.440 --> 02:22.825
Three.

02:22.865 --> 02:24.275
Rescale a characteristic,

02:24.315 --> 02:26.075
and this split stays the same.

02:26.115 --> 02:27.895
The usual one moves.

02:28.575 --> 02:29.460
Question six.

02:29.500 --> 02:30.772
The fit is unchanged.

02:30.812 --> 02:32.070
So what?

02:32.110 --> 02:35.145
Nobody uses a factor model for its R² alone.

02:35.185 --> 02:36.670
They use it to assign credit,

02:36.710 --> 02:38.180
and to hedge.

02:38.220 --> 02:38.805
Credit.

02:38.845 --> 02:40.068
For a size position,

02:40.108 --> 02:43.267
reassigning the exposure that the two sets of factors share

02:43.307 --> 02:48.255
moves the observed factors' contribution from 1.38 basis points a month

02:48.295 --> 02:50.530
to 45.53.

02:50.570 --> 02:53.565
The total stays at 2.16.

02:53.605 --> 02:54.090
Hedging.

02:54.130 --> 02:56.865
Hedge a book-to-market position with the usual ruler,

02:56.905 --> 02:59.365
and on average 46% of the hedged book,

02:59.405 --> 03:00.552
by squared weight,

03:00.592 --> 03:02.640
still sits on the model's loadings.

03:02.680 --> 03:04.227
With the characteristic ruler,

03:04.267 --> 03:05.675
none does.

03:05.715 --> 03:07.262
And in a companion paper,

03:07.302 --> 03:10.362
two normalizations with nearly identical forecasts

03:10.402 --> 03:14.575
earn Sharpe ratios of 0.69 and 1.07

03:14.615 --> 03:16.370
under the same penalty.

03:17.025 --> 03:18.010
Question seven.

03:18.050 --> 03:19.820
Do the tests change?

03:19.860 --> 03:21.420
The sharp test does not.

03:21.460 --> 03:23.595
The intercept is zero under every ruler,

03:23.635 --> 03:25.080
or under none.

03:25.120 --> 03:27.367
What changes is everything short of that:

03:27.407 --> 03:28.955
how large the intercept is,

03:28.995 --> 03:29.830
a bound on it,

03:29.870 --> 03:31.590
and the credit split.

03:31.630 --> 03:34.515
And because the ruler is estimated from the same data,

03:34.555 --> 03:37.310
the standard errors have to count that too.

03:38.090 --> 03:39.700
What the paper does not claim:

03:39.740 --> 03:41.425
that the usual ruler is wrong,

03:41.465 --> 03:43.560
or that this one earns more.

03:43.600 --> 03:45.097
The claim is narrower.

03:45.137 --> 03:48.895
Every attribution and every hedge already uses a ruler.

03:48.935 --> 03:50.365
State which one.
